---
import DeepDiveLayout from '../../layouts/DeepDiveLayout.astro';
---

<DeepDiveLayout
  title="Deep Dive into Balance"
  pillar="Balance"
  chapterNumber="02"
  nextTitle="Firing Sequence"
  nextHref="/learn/sequence"
>
  <section>
    <h2>Quick Recap</h2>

    <p>
      Balance is the ability to remain athletic throughout the load.
    </p>

    <p>
      A balanced hitter is free to move explosively in any direction without
      unnecessary compensation.
    </p>

    <p>
      Balance is not a destination. It is maintained throughout the entire
      loading movement.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Before We Begin</h2>

    <p>
      Balance is one of the most misunderstood concepts in hitting.
    </p>

    <p>
      It is not standing perfectly still.
    </p>

    <p>
      It is not keeping your weight perfectly centered.
    </p>

    <p>
      It is not avoiding movement.
    </p>

    <p>
      Balance simply means remaining athletic enough to move efficiently at
      every moment during the load.
    </p>

    <p class="callout">
      If you cannot move, you are no longer balanced.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What We Want From Balance</h2>

    <p>
      Every hitter's balance will look slightly different, but the objectives
      remain the same.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Remain athletic throughout the load.</li>
      <li>Keep the head in a neutral position.</li>
      <li>Track the baseball with both eyes.</li>
      <li>Control the body's center of mass.</li>
      <li>Stay prepared to move explosively in any direction.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Most successful hitters naturally load somewhere around
      <strong>50–60% of their weight on the back leg.</strong>
    </p>

    <p>
      The exact percentage is far less important than remaining athletic.
    </p>

    <p class="callout">
      Pressure is not weight.
    </p>

    <p>
      You can create tremendous pressure into the rear leg without allowing
      your body weight to drift over the back foot.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Head Position</h2>

    <p>
      The head should remain relatively neutral throughout the load.
    </p>

    <p>
      Excessive head movement makes tracking the baseball more difficult and
      often creates compensations later in the swing.
    </p>

    <p>
      Keep both eyes on the pitcher and allow the body to move underneath a
      quiet head whenever possible.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Common Balance Problems</h2>

    <h3>Too Much Weight on the Back Side</h3>

    <p>
      Creating pressure is good.
    </p>

    <p>
      Committing your body weight to the back side is not.
    </p>

    <h3>Collapsing the Front Side</h3>

    <p>
      The front leg should support the body throughout the load.
    </p>

    <p>
      If it collapses, energy is lost before the swing even begins.
    </p>

    <h3>Head Drift</h3>

    <p>
      Large head movements often lead to inconsistent vision and unnecessary
      adjustments later in the swing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Drills</h2>

    <div class="drill-grid">

      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Load &amp; Hold</h3>
        <p>
          Demonstrate that the load finishes in a balanced, athletic position.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Eyes Closed</h3>
        <p>
          Remove visual feedback and learn to feel true balance.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Push Test</h3>
        <p>
          Test whether the loaded position remains stable when outside force is
          applied.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Med Ball Catch</h3>
        <p>
          Catch a light medicine ball during different moments of the load
          while maintaining an athletic position.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Common Mistakes</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>Too much weight committed to the back side.</li>
      <li>Collapsing the front leg.</li>
      <li>Head drifting excessively.</li>
      <li>Standing too upright.</li>
      <li>Leaning instead of moving athletically.</li>
      <li>Confusing pressure with body weight.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="callout">
      Every movement should preserve your ability to move explosively.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Self Evaluation</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>Can I remain athletic throughout my load?</li>
      <li>Is my head relatively quiet?</li>
      <li>Can I track the baseball with both eyes?</li>
      <li>Is my weight controlled rather than committed?</li>
      <li>Can I move explosively without first regaining balance?</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

</DeepDiveLayout>